.PP
This method transforms a \fBVector<T>\fP into a logical set by: 

.PP
.PD 0
.IP "1." 4
Sorting the vector using the provided or default sorting algorithm\&.   
.IP "2." 4
Applying the comparison strategy to define the ordering of elements\&.   
.IP "3." 4
Constructing a \fBVectorSet<T, TC>\fP wrapper that interprets the sorted vector as a unique, ordered collection\&.   
.PP

.PP
Beginners: 
.br
 Think of this as “turn my vector into a sorted set”, where the library chooses sensible defaults if you don’t specify anything\&. 

.PP
Professionals: 
.br
 This method provides full control over ordering and sorting policies while maintaining predictable defaults (\fBLess<T>\fP + ShellSort)\&. 
.br
 It allows custom comparers (e\&.g\&., reverse order, length‑based, domain‑specific) and custom sorting strategies (QuickSort, CombSort, etc\&.) without forcing the user to configure them for common cases\&. 
